use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./Solid import Solid

export coding-rule SolidSrp:
    standard: @{Solid}
    ruleId: SRP
    title: Give each class only one reason to change
    statement:
        Design a class so that there is never more than one reason for it to
        change. A responsibility is a reason for change, so separate code
        that changes for different reasons, such as connection handling and
        data transfer, into different classes or at least different
        interfaces.
    rationale:
        Responsibilities placed in one class become coupled, so a change
        made for one can break the others and the design grows fragile in
        unexpected ways.
    exceptions:
        Martin notes that an axis of change matters only if changes along it
        actually occur. When hardware or platform details force
        responsibilities into one implementation class, separating their
        interfaces still keeps clients decoupled.
    severity: null
    noncompliant:
        ```java
        \\\
        class Invoice {
            Money total() { /* pricing rules */ }
            String toHtml() { /* page layout */ }
            void save(Connection db) { /* SQL */ }
        }
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```java
        \\\
        class Invoice {
            Money total() { /* pricing rules */ }
        }

        class InvoiceHtmlView {
            String render(Invoice invoice) { /* page layout */ }
        }

        class InvoiceRepository {
            void save(Invoice invoice) { /* SQL */ }
        }
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [any]
    tags: [design, cohesion, classes]
    source: https://web.archive.org/web/20110104023258/http://www.objectmentor.com/resources/articles/srp.pdf
